  "body": "## What changed\n\n- `assets/posts/tormach.cps` and `assets/posts/linuxcnc.cps` write a run-time guard before each `G10 L20` that writes X or Y.\n- The guard tests the XY rotation register of the section's work offset and of the target work offset.\n- If a rotation is not zero, the program shows a message that names the work offsets and ends with `M2`. The offset is not written.\n- A result that sets only Z has no guard. A measure-only result has no guard.\n- `linuxcnc.cps` now keeps the number of the section's work offset (`wcsIndex`), as `tormach.cps` already does.\n\n```\no903 if [[#5230 NE 0] OR [#5250 NE 0]]\n(MSG, PROBE: G54 OR G55 XY ROTATION NOT ZERO - NO OFFSET WRITTEN)\nM2\no903 endif\nG10 L20 P2 X[#5420-#190] Y[#5421-#191]\n```\n\n## Cause\n\n- The write `G10 L20 P<n> X[#5420-#190]` gives \"target = active offset + deviation\" (#2610). That proof holds for work offsets without rotation.\n- LinuxCNC keeps an XY rotation for each work offset (`R` of `G10 L2`, parameter 5210 + 20 x the system number).\n- If the target has a rotation that differs from the active system's, the written origin depends on the machine position. The target is correct at the probe position only. This is wrong by algebra (see `docs/decisions/2683-probe-rotated-wcs.md`).\n- If the two rotations are equal, the result depends on a control rule that the public LinuxCNC reference does not state: what `G10 L20` keeps for an axis that has no word. I did not confirm this rule on a control.\n- The post cannot read the rotation at post time. Only the program can test it.\n\nThe issue said that the defect also applies when the target is the active work offset. I did not confirm that part. The guard also covers it, because the rule is not stated. The decision file says how to relax the guard after a run on a control.\n\n## Tests\n\nNew file `crates/post/tests/probe_rotated_wcs.rs`. Each test runs the two posts.\n\n- `setting_the_active_offset_stops_when_it_is_rotated`\n- `setting_another_offset_stops_when_either_is_rotated`\n- `the_guard_follows_the_sections_work_offset`\n- `the_guard_is_written_once_under_its_own_o_number`\n- `a_z_only_result_and_a_measure_only_result_have_no_guard`\n\nRed before the fix: yes. On unchanged code the three guard tests failed, because no guard line was before `G10 L20`.\n\nMutation results:\n\n- Old `tormach.cps` from `upstream/main`: 3 tests red. Restored: green.\n- Old `linuxcnc.cps` from `upstream/main`: 3 tests red. Restored: green.\n- `wcsIndex = n;` removed from `linuxcnc.cps`: `the_guard_follows_the_sections_work_offset` red. Restored: green.\n- `if (xy)` changed to `if (true)` in `tormach.cps`: `a_z_only_result_and_a_measure_only_result_have_no_guard` red. Restored: green.\n\nGate, on base `3a240b7fb` (the `upstream/main` of the start of the work):\n\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main`: 653 steps, 0 failed, 36.9 min. The changed shared golden made the plan run each app suite.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-post --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean. No Rust file of another crate changed.\n- `upstream/main` moved to `75e74a9cb` during the gate. `git merge-tree` shows a clean merge, and no commit in that range changes a file of this PR. I did not run the gate again on the new base.\n\n## Goldens moved\n\nSix goldens changed. Each one got the same four guard lines directly before its `G10 L20` line, and no other line changed (`git diff upstream/main -- \"*.nc\"`: 24 insertions, 0 deletions).\n\n- `crates/app/tests/golden/nc/tormach/probe_edge.nc` (`o902`)\n- `crates/app/tests/golden/nc/tormach/probe_corner.nc` (`o903`)\n- `crates/app/tests/golden/nc/tormach/probe_bore_centre.nc` (`o903`)\n- `crates/app/tests/golden/nc/tormach/probe_boss_centre.nc` (`o903`)\n- `crates/app/tests/golden/nc/tormach/probe_bore_centre_mm.nc` (`o903`)\n- `crates/post/tests/recorded/golden/tormach/probe.nc` (`o903`)\n\n`probe_face.nc` did not change, because a face result sets only Z.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- No program ran on LinuxCNC, PathPilot or PathPilot Hub. The words `NE` and `OR`, and the parameters `#5230`.., are from the LinuxCNC G-code reference as I know it. I could not open the reference from this machine (the network call was not permitted).\n- No test runs the program in a controller model. #2682 owns that model.\n- The guard message was first `... OFFSET NOT SET`. `probe_tormach.rs` reads each word that starts with `S` as a spindle word, so I changed the text and did not change that test.\n\n## Overlaps\n\nNone. No open PR changes `tormach.cps`, `linuxcnc.cps` or the probe tests.\n\nChangelog: The Tormach and LinuxCNC probe programs stop with a message, and write no work offset, when a work offset that they use has an XY rotation.\n\nEffort: high\n\nFixes #2683\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
